# Line endings are pinned so every checkout's working tree matches the index.
#
# This used to matter to the release gate, which once digested the bytes of
# every tracked file on disk: a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true
# produced CRLF working files and a different digest from the same commit, and
# the rendered-fidelity sign-off was rejected for a reason that had nothing to
# do with what was reviewed. scripts/hash-release-inputs.mjs now digests the
# commit's tree (`git ls-tree -r HEAD`), so release_inputs_sha256 is a property
# of the commit and line endings can no longer move it.
#
# eol=lf stays for working-tree consistency: scripts, fixtures, and tests read
# these files as bytes, and a tool should see the same file on every machine.
# Nothing in the repository changes: the index is already LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted. text=auto already detects
# these, but the release digest and the pinned SHA-256 checks in
# scripts/verify-assets.mjs make silent corruption expensive enough to be
# explicit about it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.gguf binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.zip binary
*.traineddata binary
